\section{Method}

\subsection{Study Design}

We conducted a two-phase qualitative household study in Bangladesh between January and June 2026. Phase 1 examined existing medication practices through home interviews and routine walkthroughs with older adults and family members involved in their medication management. We analysed this material before developing a prototype, deployed the prototype for two weeks, and then conducted post-deployment interviews.

We treated the household as the primary context of medication management because family members often participated in purchasing, reading, organizing, reminding, or checking medicines. Where a family caregiver was involved, we recruited them as a participant in their own right. When both an older adult and caregiver discussed the same event, we retained both accounts rather than combining them into a single household account.

All sessions took place in participants' homes and were conducted in Bangla. Participants came from urban and rural settings.

\subsubsection{Phase 1: Formative Home Interviews}

Phase 1 examined how medication work was organized within participants' existing routines. We used separate semi-structured guides for older adults and caregivers covering daily medication routines, storage, disruptions, missed or delayed doses, reading and interaction conditions, family involvement, division of responsibilities, and handover when the person usually responsible was unavailable.

We asked participants to describe recent instances and demonstrate parts of their routine rather than relying only on general descriptions. We therefore approached medication management as situated practice rather than solely as individual preference \cite{61,9}.

The caregiver guide focused on caregivers' own work, including how responsibilities became theirs, what happened when they were unavailable, and how they determined whether a dose had been taken. During transcription, we distinguished caregivers' accounts of their own activities from descriptions of an older adult's experience and from jointly described household events \cite{106}.

Each session followed the same general sequence: consent, observation of the immediate setting, semi-structured interview, medication-routine walkthrough, and closing design reflection. Sessions were audio-recorded in Bangla with consent. Researchers also recorded contextual observations, including medicine storage, relevant lighting and noise conditions, and household members involved during the walkthrough.

Phase 1 informed our analysis of existing medication practices and the design requirements for the prototype. It does not provide evidence about interaction with the prototype.

\subsubsection{Prototype Development}

We developed the prototype after analysing Phase 1 rather than beginning with a fixed feature set. The analysis informed requirements concerning timely reminders, medicine and timing information, reduced reliance on manually consulting a schedule, family awareness of unconfirmed doses, simplified interaction, readable information, and opportunities for family involvement.

The deployed prototype allowed participants or family members to configure medication schedules manually. It delivered scheduled reminders containing the medicine name and timing and provided a logbook, streak, and daily heat map. When a dose remained unconfirmed, the system could ask the older adult whether a family member should be notified. Participants could decline this request. Medication schedules and changes in whom the system served remained under human control.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figures/initial-uses.jpg}
  \Description{Four-panel storyboard of the design concept: capturing a prescription, the schedule being set from it, a spoken reminder at dose time, and a routine the person can follow.}
  \caption{\textbf{Design concept developed from the Phase 1 analysis.} The concept included prescription capture, schedule creation, spoken reminders, and medication routines organized around those reminders. Prescription capture was specified but was not implemented in the deployed prototype.}
  \label{fig:design-concept}
\end{figure}

The streak and heat map were designed to make medication records visible within the household and potentially provide occasions for family interaction. We treat this as a design rationale rather than an observed effect; participants' responses to these features are reported in the Findings.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figures/relational-trigger-loop.png}
  \Description{Loop diagram: the agent reminds, the dose is taken, a streak and heat map record it, a family member sees the record and says something, and the routine returns to the household. A dashed branch shows an unconfirmed dose reaching the family only after the agent asks and the older adult grants.}
  \caption{\textbf{Interaction loop in the deployed prototype.} After a scheduled reminder, an older adult could confirm taking the dose, updating the medication record, streak, and heat map. An unconfirmed dose could enter the family-notification path after the older adult granted permission.}
  \label{fig:trigger-loop}
\end{figure}

The broader design specification contained four mechanisms that were not implemented in the deployed prototype: a risk-graded weakening veto, patterned interpretation of silence, a probationary onboarding mode, and shared family scores. Phase 2 responses concerning these mechanisms are treated only as reactions to described design concepts.

\subsubsection{Phase 2: Post-Deployment Interviews}

We returned after the two-week deployment and conducted interviews in participants' homes and in Bangla. When both an older adult and an enrolled caregiver participated, each discussed the same deployment period from their own perspective.

The Phase 2 guide distinguished between participants' accounts of features they had used and their responses to the four unimplemented mechanisms. Questions about deployed features covered reminders, announcements of whom the system was serving, requests to involve a family member, refusals, family notifications, and medication records.

Some questions about unimplemented mechanisms were phrased as though participants had experienced them. We therefore did not treat agreement with these prompts as evidence of use. We also distinguished episodes or interpretations volunteered by participants from agreement expressed after a probe introduced an interpretation. Prompted agreement alone was treated as weaker evidence during analysis.

Accordingly, deployed features support claims about participants' reported experiences during the two-week study. Responses to unimplemented mechanisms support only claims about how participants reasoned about those proposed designs. Neither provides evidence about clinical outcomes or sustained use beyond the deployment.

\subsection{Participants and Recruitment}

We recruited through snowball sampling. Initial contacts came through the research team's social networks, after which participants referred potentially eligible people through friends, colleagues, and community groups. We approached approximately 30 households.

Older adults were eligible if they were at least 65 years old and took medication daily. Family members were eligible if they participated in medication-related work for an older adult living in the same household. Eligibility information was collected during screening and discussed again during the home visit. Participation was voluntary and unpaid.

The final sample comprised 25 participants: 17 older adults and eight family caregivers. Sixteen older adults participated in both phases and one participated only in Phase 2. Seven caregivers participated in both phases and one participated only in Phase 1.

Older adults were aged 65--80 years (mean = 69.4; median = 68); nine were women and eight were men. Eleven reported needing large text, including two who could not read. Two described high smartphone comfort, while nine described low or very low comfort. Nine used their own smartphone during deployment and five used a household smartphone shared with or operated by a relative; device information was not recorded for three participants.

The eight caregivers included four women and four men. Six reported their age, ranging from 20 to 31 years, and included daughters, sons, a daughter-in-law, and grandchildren.

