\subsection{Deployment Procedure}

Researchers installed the application during a home visit and configured the initial medication schedule with the older adult or, where relevant, the family member operating the household device. Onboarding covered reminders, the logbook, and the system's spoken announcement of whom it was serving.

Shared or intermediated device use was part of the household context rather than being treated as equivalent to independent smartphone use. Similar forms of intermediated technology use have been documented in the region \cite{98,33,1}.

The prototype was used for two weeks before the Phase 2 interviews.

\subsection{Transcription and Translation}

The verified Bangla transcripts served as the analytic record. Automatic speech recognition produced initial Bangla transcripts, and machine translation produced initial English renderings. Neither automated output was treated as final.

Two Bangla-fluent researchers verified each complete Bangla transcript against the audio and checked the English rendering against the verified Bangla version. Coding and interpretation were conducted against the Bangla records. We returned to the Bangla transcript when kinship terms, honorifics, or relational expressions were not adequately represented in English.

Automated systems were used only to produce preliminary transcription and translation drafts. They were not used for coding, theme development, or interpretation.

\subsection{Data Analysis}

We analysed both phases using reflexive thematic analysis \cite{15,16}. Coding began inductively and was later considered in relation to the research questions. We worked at semantic and latent levels where relevant and treated themes as researcher-constructed interpretations rather than entities that independently emerged from the data.

Analysis began with repeated reading and memoing. The two interviewing researchers first familiarized themselves with the transcripts and developed initial codes. Coding and interpretation then developed iteratively through discussion with the supervising researcher.

We did not calculate inter-rater reliability because agreement between coders was not used as a criterion for theme validity within our reflexive thematic approach \cite{16,93}. Differences in interpretation instead informed analytic discussion.

We clustered related codes into candidate themes and repeatedly checked them against the transcripts. Themes were revised, bounded, combined, separated, or renamed during this process. We returned to the Bangla record whenever an interpretation depended on language that was not adequately carried into English.

We kept Phase 1 interpretations, resulting design decisions, and Phase 2 responses analytically distinguishable. Responses concerning unimplemented mechanisms were additionally marked as judgments about proposed designs. When older adults and caregivers described the same household event differently, we retained that divergence as analytic material rather than resolving it into a single account.

We maintained analytic memos, coding records, theme maps, and records of cases that complicated developing interpretations. We make no claim of thematic saturation.

\subsection{Ethical Considerations}

\subsubsection{Consent}

Researchers read the study information aloud before requesting consent, covering the study purpose, procedures, recording, transcript use, and data handling. This allowed participants who could not read to receive the same information verbally.

Consent was obtained individually. Caregivers consented separately from the older adults whose routines they might discuss, and consent for audio recording was requested separately. Participants were informed that participation was voluntary and that they could discontinue participation. Because family members were sometimes present during home sessions, we recorded who was present during the consent process \cite{44,71}.

\subsubsection{Data Handling and Privacy}

Participants were informed that recordings would undergo automated transcription and translation followed by human verification. Personal names were replaced with participant identifiers, and the identifier key was stored separately from the analytic corpus.

Medication-related accounts could also contain information about other household members. During analysis, we therefore distinguished participants' own experiences from descriptions of another person's activities and from jointly described household events.

\subsubsection{Researcher Position}

Three researchers conducted the study: two conducted interviews, transcript verification, and coding, while a third supervised the analysis. All three are fluent in Bangla and English, allowing interviews, consent, verification, and analysis to proceed without an interpreter.

Our position shaped both recruitment and interpretation. Recruitment began through the research team's social networks, making some households more reachable than others. Language fluency also led us to retain Bangla transcripts as the primary analytic record rather than treating English translations as the source for interpretation \cite{103,75}.

\subsubsection{Scope of the Evidence}

The findings concern medication practices and short-term prototype use within the participating Bangladeshi households. Snowball sampling through the research team's networks does not support population-level claims about older adults or caregiving households in Bangladesh.

The two-week deployment supports claims about participants' reported experiences during that period, not longer-term use or changes following shifts in medication regimens or household responsibilities.

We did not measure adherence, medication errors, or health outcomes and therefore make no claims that the prototype changed them. Responses to the four unimplemented mechanisms are similarly limited to participants' judgments about described designs rather than observed use.
